## Comments inside JSX

JSX has no comment syntax of its own. Inside markup you open a JavaScript expression with braces and put a block comment in it:

```jsx
function Greeting() {
  return (
    <div>
      {/* this is a JSX comment */}
      <h1>Hello</h1>
      {/* comments can
          span multiple lines too */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

The `{}` is a normal expression slot, and a block comment evaluates to nothing, so React renders nothing for it.

## Why a plain // does not work

A `//` line comment placed directly in markup is treated as text and rendered on the page. Even inside braces it is risky, because the closing `}` can end up commented out — keep comments to the block form in JSX.

## Comments in component logic

Outside the returned JSX — the function body, hooks, handlers — it is ordinary JavaScript, so normal `//` and block comments work as usual:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  // plain JS comment — fine here
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

## Comment with intent

The best comment explains why, not what — the markup already says what. A note on why a workaround exists, or why an element is conditionally hidden, is worth far more than one restating the tag.

