## What it is

The virtual DOM is a tree of plain JavaScript objects that mirrors the structure of the real DOM. Each node records a type, props, and children — cheap to create and cheap to discard, unlike real DOM nodes.

## The update cycle

When a component renders, React produces a new virtual tree. It compares that tree against the previous one — diffing — to work out exactly what changed, then commits only those differences to the real DOM:

```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [n, setN] = useState(0);
  return <p>Count: {n}</p>;
}
// setN(1) -> new virtual tree -> diff finds only the text changed
// -> React updates just that one text node
```

## Why the indirection helps

Real DOM operations are expensive — layout, reflow, repaint — and mutating the DOM by hand is error-prone. The virtual DOM lets you re-describe the whole UI on every state change as if it were cheap, and React derives the minimal real update for you.

## The honest caveat

The virtual DOM is not magically fast — it trades some diffing work for a much simpler programming model. Frameworks like Svelte skip it by compiling updates ahead of time. For React, the real performance lever is rendering less: stable keys, memoisation, and not putting derivable data in state.

